Z||000000||danny boy||04-08-2001||10:34 PM|||| I finished up my new suspension setup and brakes today and wow! the car is a whole different animal now! I put the stock springs back on the front and took off the steering stabilizer. I took the 1" front swaybar I had and mounted it on the back, ran the coilovers all the way up so the car sits stock height(looks funny after seeing it 3"lower)with 250# springs. The "rear" bar is using urethane bushings and rubber endlink bushings. the brakes are stock front calipers all the way around with new pads. Since I broke one of the rear calipers trying to take it apart I just used a set of fronts.Less unsprung weight anyway.<BR>This thing handles very good. I'm venturing to say Stealth good. No more rear sway AT ALL.<BR>Next will be Urethane rear control arm bushings and front end links.And a tire/wheel upgrade is in the near future.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-09-2001||10:45 AM||||Sounds great, I hope someday I'll have enough cash to redo my suspension.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||danny boy||04-09-2001||05:25 PM|||| I highly recommend using a front 1" swaybar on the rear. If not, at least a WS6 front bar. The 3/4" rear bar was way too small.I'm not sure what size the Addco bar is, but the one I had was Koni(Fiero Store).||||reg||1
